Treatment-Induced Myelosuppression and Febrile Neutropenia
Haematology & Oncology
Fever >38.3C post-chemotherapy, Absolute neutrophil count <0.5 x 10^9/L, Spontaneous mucosal bleeding, Absence of localized inflammatory signs

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-046
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of high fever and severe, shaking rigors in an actively treated oncology patient, requiring urgent assessment for overwhelming neutropenic sepsis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient precisely ten days post-cytotoxic chemotherapy who developed a sudden, sustained high fever and extreme lethargy over the last two hours. Urgent in-person evaluation was strictly required to rule out rapidly progressive bacteraemia and hypovolaemic shock, which carried a catastrophic mortality risk if unassessed prior to the next morning clinic.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I feel incredibly weak, my teeth will not stop chattering from the cold, and my gums just started bleeding for no reason."
History of Present Illness: The 55-year-old patient reports an insidious onset of extreme generalized fatigue and profound physical weakness starting yesterday, precisely ten days following their last cycle of systemic cytotoxic chemotherapy. Two hours ago, they experienced a sudden onset of severe, shaking rigors followed by a sustained oral temperature of 38.6C. They report new, spontaneous mucosal bleeding from the gums and epistaxis tonight. There is a complete absence of localized pain, cough, dysuria, or purulent discharge.
Functional Status: Bedbound and actively shivering; entirely dependent on their spouse for oral fluids.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living thirty minutes from the nearest tertiary oncology center; high risk of rapid cardiovascular collapse en route.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 95/55 | HR: 125 | Temp: 38.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 97 percent | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Pale, toxic-appearing adult in acute distress, experiencing severe rigors and marked diaphoresis.
Physical Exam:
HEENT: Active, slow oozing of venous blood from the gingival margins. Exquisitely painful, deep mucosal ulcerations (stomatitis) observed on the buccal mucosa. Complete absence of localized erythema or purulent exudate around the ulcers, reflecting a profound lack of neutrophils.
Skin: Widespread, non-blanching purpura noted on the bilateral forearms. Central venous catheter site is visually clean with absolute lack of surrounding erythema or fluctuance.
Cardiovascular: Tachycardic, hyperdynamic circulation. Capillary refill is delayed at 4 seconds. Orthostatic hypotension present on attempted sitting.
Respiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally. Tachypnoeic secondary to fever.
Environmental Observations: Multiple thick blankets piled on the patient.
Medication Reconciliation: Took paracetamol 1000mg two hours ago with absolute zero effect on the fever curve.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Febrile Neutropenia - Status: Exacerbated (Impending Septic Shock)
Differentials:

Viral Upper Respiratory Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the profound absence of coryzal symptoms, the exact temporal correlation to the expected chemotherapy nadir, and the presence of severe rigors and hypotension indicating a massive bacterial systemic inflammatory response.

Central Line-Associated Bloodstream Infection (CLABSI) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the visually uninflamed line site, though the absolute lack of neutrophils completely masks local infection signs, meaning the line remains the most highly probable source of the suspected bacteraemia.

Acute Leukaemia Transformation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the known, predictable iatrogenic cause of the cytopenia (recent cytotoxic drugs) rather than a spontaneous primary bone marrow failure.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, terrifying triad of febrile neutropenia: high fever, severe rigors, and severe cytopenias directly following cytotoxic chemotherapy. The tachycardia and evolving hypotension indicate early, uncompensated septic shock driven by suspected overwhelming bacteraemia. Because the patient lacks the neutrophils necessary to mount a localized inflammatory response, the systemic signs are the only warning of impending cardiovascular collapse. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would guarantee fatal septic shock overnight. This medical emergency unequivocally mandated an urgent after-hours visit to recognize the sepsis cascade, perform initial resuscitation, and orchestrate immediate, rapid-priority hospital transfer for broad-spectrum intravenous antibiotics.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate dispatch of a priority ambulance for transfer to the tertiary Emergency Department. Handover provided specifying the exact chemotherapy protocol, the day of the cycle (Day 10 nadir), and the critical requirement for immediate blood cultures and empirical intravenous piperacillin-tazobactam within sixty minutes of arrival.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain strictly lying flat in the bed without pillows until the paramedics arrive. By minimizing the gravitational challenge to your heart, you preserve essential blood flow to your brain and kidneys while your blood pressure is dangerously low.

Instruct your spouse to remove all the heavy blankets despite the shivering. By exposing your skin to the ambient air, you physically facilitate heat loss, preventing your core temperature from rising to levels that cause brain damage.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

